  "notes": "The most straightforwardly *pop* female country act of the mid-2010s — synth-forward, hook-dense, teen-radio-shaped. Drums are \"programmed-first with clap/snap backbeat, 16th hats, sampled kick, and a full drop-out before the final chorus,\" and the entry's most distinctive claim is a timbre used as rhythm: \"banjo or mandolin plucks used as *percussive* 16ths,\" which is a dense perc lane rather than a melodic one. The 808 block is authored because the entry states the low end explicitly — \"synth sub carrying the root under a simple electric bass; the sub is the low end, not the bass guitar\" — in the `counter_riff` role so the simple electric bass part survives above it. Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V as two-bar loops with pads,\" which is why the harmonic rhythm leans on `2_bars_per_chord` and only two families carry real weight. Melody is \"bright soprano, syllabically dense, 16th-note phrasing, wide chorus leaps, layered doubles and stacked harmonies as production texture\" — the highest female register in this batch with a real leap share. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF] and the entry notes the signature single reads either ~128 or ~76 at the half. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
